Answer: If you create a wrapper class, this will work with any other class, either built-in or not.  This is called "containment and delegation", and it is a common alternative to inheritance: [CODE] class SuperDuperWrapper(object):
    def __init__(self, origobj):
        self.myobj = origobj
    def __str__(self):
        return "SUPER DUPER " + str(self.myobj)
    def __getattr__(self,attr):
        return getattr(self.myobj, attr)
 [/CODE] The [CODE] __getattr__ [/CODE] method will delegate all undefined attribute requests on your SuperDuperWrapper object to the contained myobj object.  In fact, given Python's dynamic typing, you could use this class to SuperDuper'ly wrap just about anything: [CODE] s = "hey ho!"
sds = SuperDuperWrapper(s)
print sds

i = 100
sdi = SuperDuperWrapper(i)
print sdi
 [/CODE] Prints: [CODE] SUPER DUPER hey ho!
SUPER DUPER 100
 [/CODE] In your case, you would take the returned object from the function you cannot modify, and wrap it in your own SuperDuperWrapper, but you could still otherwise access it just as if it were the base object. [CODE] print sds.split()
['hey', 'ho!']
 [/CODE]

answer:
What you're looking for is called a "metaclass"... just like [CODE] a [/CODE] is an instance of class [CODE] A [/CODE] , [CODE] A [/CODE] is an instance of class as well, referred to as a metaclass. By default, Python  classes are instances of the [CODE] type [/CODE] class (the only exception is under Python 2, which has some legacy "old style" classes, which are those which don't inherit from [CODE] object [/CODE] ). You can check this by doing [CODE] type(A) [/CODE] ... it should return [CODE] type [/CODE] itself (yes, that object has been overloaded a little bit). Metaclasses are powerful and brain-twisting enough to deserve more than the quick explanation I was about to write... a good starting point would be this stackoverflow question: What is a Metaclass . For your particular question, for Python 2, the following creates a metaclass which aliases [CODE] len(A) [/CODE] to invoke a class method on A: [CODE] class LengthMetaclass(type):

    def __len__(self):
        return self.clslength()

class A(object):
    __metaclass__ = LengthMetaclass

    @classmethod
    def clslength(cls):
        return 7

print len(A)
 [/CODE] Python 3 works similarly, but you would use [CODE] class A(object, metaclass=LengthMetaclass): [/CODE] instead of [CODE] __metaclass__ = LengthMetaclass [/CODE] . The reason [CODE] LengthMetaclass.__len__ [/CODE] doesn't affect instances of [CODE] A [/CODE] is that attribute resolution in Python first checks the instance dict, then walks the class hierarchy [CODE] [A, object] [/CODE] , but it never consults the metaclasses. Whereas accessing [CODE] A.__len__ [/CODE] first consults the instance [CODE] A [/CODE] , then walks it's class hierarchy, which consists of [CODE] [LengthMetaclass, type] [/CODE] .

