Operators for new-style classes Could anyone explain to me why [CODE] A()+A() [/CODE] does give an error, but [CODE] B()+B() [/CODE] works as expected? I came across this when I was writing a larger piece of code, but this seems to be the smallest code necessary to reproduce it. [CODE] from types import MethodType

D = {'__add__': lambda x, y: "".join((repr(x), repr(y)))}

class A(object):
    def __getattr__(self, item):
        if item == '__coerce__':
            raise AttributeError()
        return MethodType(D[item], self)
    def __repr__(self):
        return "A"

class B():
    def __getattr__(self, item):
        if item == '__coerce__':
            raise AttributeError()
        return MethodType(D[item], self)
    def __repr__(self):
        return "B"

try:
    A()+A()
except Exception as e:
    print e

B()+B()
 [/CODE] Does anyone have an explanation?

Answer: That's because new style classes never invoke [CODE] __coerce__ [/CODE] with binary operators. Also for special methods [CODE] __getattr__ [/CODE] is never invoked in new style classes: From Data model coercion rules : New-style classes (those derived from object) never invoke the [CODE] __coerce__() [/CODE] method in response to a binary operator; the only time [CODE] __coerce__() [/CODE] is invoked is when the built-in function [CODE] coerce() [/CODE] is called.

