question:
Python list, lookup object name, efficiency advice Suppose I have the following object: [CODE] class Foo(object):
  def __init__(self, name=None):
    self.name = name

  def __repr__(self):
    return self.name
 [/CODE] And a list containing multiple instances, such as: [CODE] list = [Foo(name='alice'), Foo(name='bob'), Foo(name='charlie')]
 [/CODE] If I want to find an object with a given name, I could use the following: [CODE] def get_by_name(name, list):
  return [foo for foo in list if foo.name == name][-1]
 [/CODE] Which would obviously mean: [CODE] print get_by_name('alice', list)
>> alice
 [/CODE] However, is there a more efficient data structure or method for retrieving such objects? In reality, the object names are only known at runtime and could in theory change throughout the lifecycle of the object. Any advice? UPDATE: Thanks to Matt Joiners answer, I have updated it to support multiple Foo 's with the same name: [CODE] class Foo(object):
    _all_names = {}    
    def __init__(self, name=None):
        self._name = None
        self.name = name        
    @property
    def name(self):
        return self._name        
    @name.setter
    def name(self, name):
        if self._name is not None:
            self._all_names[self._name].remove(self)
        self._name = name
        if name is not None:
            self._all_names.setdefault(name, []).append(self)
    @classmethod
    def get_by_name(cls, name):
        return cls._all_names[name]        
    def __repr__(self):
        return "{0}".format(self.name)

l = [Foo("alice"), Foo("bob"), Foo('alice'), Foo('charlie')]
print Foo.get_by_name("alice")
print Foo.get_by_name("charlie")
 [/CODE] Any comments on this approach?

answer:
Try this for size: [CODE] class Foo(object):
    _all_names = {}
    def __init__(self, name=None):
        self.name = name
    @property
    def name(self):
        return self._name
    @name.setter
    def name(self, name):
        self._name = name
        self._all_names[name] = self
    @classmethod
    def get_by_name(cls, name):
        return cls._all_names[name]
    def __str__(self):
        return "Foo({0})".format(self.name)

a = Foo("alice")
b = Foo("bob")
print Foo.get_by_name("alice")
 [/CODE] Keep in mind it's kept minimal to convey the idea, there are many tweaks and checks you can make here and there.

