Why can't `round` be defined for non-floats? Given a simple class like [CODE] class Vector(object):
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value
    def __abs__(self):
        return math.sqrt(sum([x**2 for x in self.value]))
    def __round__(self, *n):
        return [round(x,*n) for x in self.value]
 [/CODE] why does [CODE] abs(Vector([-3,4])) [/CODE] correctly yield [CODE] 5 [/CODE] while [CODE] round(Vector([-3.1,4])) [/CODE] complains with a [CODE] TypeError: a float is required [/CODE] instead of the desired [CODE] [-3,4] [/CODE] , and how can this be fixed? I know [CODE] round [/CODE] should usually return a float, but for a vector as in this example there is probably no ambiguity on the possible meaning, so why can this not be simply overridden? Do I really have to subclass [CODE] numbers.Real [/CODE] , or define [CODE] Vector(...).round(n) [/CODE] instead?

answer:
The [CODE] __round__ [/CODE] special method was only introduced in Python 3. There is no support for the special method in Python 2. You'll have to use a dedicated method instead of the function: [CODE] class Vector(object):
    def __init__(self, value):
        self.value = value

    def round(self, n):
        return [round(x, n) for x in self.value]
 [/CODE] or you'd have to provide your own [CODE] round() [/CODE] function: [CODE] import __builtin__

def round(number, digits=0):
    try:
        return number.__round__(digits)
    except AttributeError:
        return __builtin__.round(number, digits)
 [/CODE] You could even monkey-patch this into the [CODE] __builtins__ [/CODE] namespace: [CODE] import __builtin__

_bltin_round = __builtin__.round

def round(number, digits=0):
    try:
        hook = number.__round__
    except AttributeError:
        return _bltin_round(number, digits)
    else:
        # Call hook outside the exception handler so an AttributeError 
        # thrown by its implementation is not masked
        return hook(digits)

__builtin__.round = round
 [/CODE]

