question:
Python equivalent of Java's compareTo() I'm doing a project in Python (3.2) for which I need to compare user defined objects.  I'm used to OOP in Java, where one would define a [CODE] compareTo() [/CODE] method in the class that specifies the natural ordering of that class, as in the example below: [CODE] public class Foo {
    int a, b;

    public Foo(int aa, int bb) {
        a = aa;
        b = bb;
    }

    public int compareTo(Foo that) {
        // return a negative number if this < that
        // return 0 if this == that
        // return a positive number if this > that

        if (this.a == that.a) return this.b - that.b;
        else return this.a - that.a;
    }
}
 [/CODE] I'm fairly new to classes/objects in Python, so I'd like to know what is the "pythonic" way to define the natural ordering of a class?

answer:
You can implement the special methods [CODE] __lt__ [/CODE] , [CODE] __gt__ [/CODE] etc. to implement the default operators for custom types. See more about them in the language reference . For example: [CODE] class Foo:
    def __init__ (self, a, b):
        self.a = a
        self.b = b

    def __lt__ (self, other):
        if self.a == other.a:
            return self.b < other.b
        return self.a < other.b

    def __gt__ (self, other):
        return other.__lt__(self)

    def __eq__ (self, other):
        return self.a == other.b and self.b == other.b

    def __ne__ (self, other):
        return not self.__eq__(other)
 [/CODE] Or as said by stranac in the comments, you can use the [CODE] total_ordering [/CODE] decorator to save some typing: [CODE] @functools.total_ordering
class Foo:
    def __init__ (self, a, b):
        self.a = a
        self.b = b

    def __lt__ (self, other):
        if self.a == other.a:
            return self.b < other.b
        return self.a < other.b

    def __eq__ (self, other):
        return self.a == other.b and self.b == other.b
 [/CODE]

