question:
Go - What is the equivalent of Python's "pass"? I have a default cause in a select statement that I want to do nothing, just continue, but leaving the line blank stops anything in the statement from happening [CODE]         select {
        case quit_status := <-quit:
            if quit_status == true {
                fmt.Printf("********************* GOROUTINE [%d] Received QUIT MSG\n", id)
                return
            }
        default:
            fmt.Printf("GOROUTINE [%d] step: %d, NO QUIT MSG\n", id, i)
        }
 [/CODE]

answer:
The [CODE] default [/CODE] case in a [CODE] select [/CODE] statement is intended to provide non-blocking I/O for channel reads and writes. The code in the [CODE] default [/CODE] case is executed whenever none of the channels in any of the cases are ready to be read/written to. So in your case, the [CODE] default [/CODE] block is executed if the quit channel has nothing to say.
You can simply remove the default case and it will block on the [CODE] quit_status := <-quit [/CODE] case until a value is available in [CODE] quit [/CODE] .. which is probably what you are after in this instance. If you want to immediately continue executing code after the select statement, you should run this select statement in a separate goroutine: [CODE] go func() {
    select {
    case quit_status := <-quit:
        ...

    }
}()

// Execution continues here immediately.
 [/CODE]

