answer:
I use a nested function to find matches from a list: [CODE] def get_exact(data, key, match):
    def is_match(item):
        if (key in item) and (item[key].lower() == match.lower()):
            return item
        return False
    return [i for i in data if is_match(i)]
 [/CODE] No other calls in the project have a need to use is_match(item), so why declare it separately? However, I will say that, for my example, declaring is_match() outside of get_exact() does run around [CODE] ~0.04 seconds [/CODE] faster in 10,000 iterations. [CODE] def is_match(item, key, match):
    if (key in item) and (item[key].lower() == match.lower()):
        return item
    return False

def get_exact(data, key, match):
    return [i for i in data if is_match(i, key, match)]
 [/CODE]

