Adapting the idea of @jochen I came to this: extract the full circle mask as you've done (I called it [CODE] fullForeground [/CODE] ) from your colored image, compute grayscale, blur (median blur size 7) it and and extract edges, for example with [CODE] cv::Laplacian [/CODE] This laplacian thresholded > 50 gives: [CODE] cv::Laplacian(blurred, lap, 0, 5); // no delta [/CODE] [CODE] lapMask = lap > 50; // thresholding to values > 50 [/CODE] This one dilated once gives: [CODE] cv::dilate(lapMask, dilatedThresholdedLaplacian, cv::Mat()); // dilate the edge mask once
 [/CODE] Now subtraction [CODE] fullForeground - dilatedThresholdedLaplacian [/CODE] (same as and_not operator for this type of masks) gives: from this you can compute contours. For each contour you can compute the area and compare it to the area of an enclosing circle, giving this code and result: [CODE] std::vector<std::vector<cv::Point> > contours;
cv::findContours(separated.clone(), contours, CV_RETR_LIST, CV_CHAIN_APPROX_NONE);

double minArea = 500;
double minCircleRatio = 0.5;
for(unsigned int i=0; i<contours.size(); ++i)
{
    double cArea = cv::contourArea(contours[i]);
    if(cArea < minArea) continue;

