How do libraries in different programming languages handle Date & Time, Timestamps & Durations, Leapseconds & -years, DSTs & Timezones, ...? Is there a standard body or a specific normative way how time-related things should be implemented in practice (like ICU for Unicode-related tasks) or is this currently a "best-effort", depending on how much effort, time and money language and library implementers want to spend? Is there a specific and complete implementation which could serve as a example for how time-related things should be handled? Which existing library would you consider as a bad, a decent or a good example?

Answer: I'll try to give an answer to the second and third question using the Java library which might become part of Java 7. javax.time.* (JSR 310) These classes are a complete rewrite of JodaTime trying to fix the design flaws of [CODE] util.Date [/CODE] / [CODE] util.Time [/CODE] as well as JodaTime. JSR 310 tries to provide a comprehensive model for date and time, which is type-safe and self-documenting. It is interoperable with existing classes, but also considers XML- and DBMS-based use-cases.
The classes are final, immutable, thread-safe and cannot be modified after construction. Instances are created via a rich set of Factory methods which can cache things in the background. [CODE] LocalDate dateToday     = LocalDate.of(2010, 9, 14);
LocalDate oneMonthLater = dateToday.with(OCTOBER);
LocalDate oneYearLater  = dateToday.withYear(2011);
 [/CODE] The API has some "machine-oriented" classes and some "human-oriented" classes: Machine-oriented [CODE] Instant [/CODE] For a point of time comparable to an Unix or Java timestamp. Actually there are [CODE] Instant [/CODE] , [CODE] TAIInstant [/CODE] and [CODE] UTCInstant [/CODE] which enable people to exactly choose which definition of time they need i. e. "day-based", "linear, without leap seconds" etc. [CODE] Duration [/CODE] A time range not necessarily associated with a specific Date or Calendar. Human-oriented There is a rich collection of classes handling different use-cases like Date-only, Time-only, Time and Date, with and without Timezones, with and without DST. [CODE] DateProvider [/CODE] [CODE] OffsetDate [/CODE] , [CODE] LocalDate [/CODE] (, [CODE] java.sql.Date [/CODE] compatibility) [CODE] TimeProvider [/CODE] [CODE] OffsetTime [/CODE] , [CODE] LocalTime [/CODE] (, [CODE] java.sql.Time [/CODE] compatibility) [CODE] DateTimeProvider [/CODE] [CODE] ZonedDateTime [/CODE] , [CODE] OffsetDateTime [/CODE] , [CODE] LocalDateTime [/CODE] (, [CODE] java.util.GregorianCalendar [/CODE] compatibility) [CODE] InstantProvider

