Server side events are a sort of http protocol; and you may have any number of concurrent http requests in flight at any given moment, you may have zero if nobody is connected, or dozens.  This nuance is all wrapped up in the two [CODE] sse.serve [/CODE] and [CODE] sse.Handler [/CODE] constructs;  the former represents a single listening port, which dispatches each separate client request to the latter. Additionally, [CODE] sse.Handler.handle_request() [/CODE] is called once for each client, and the client is disconnected once that co-routine terminates.  In your code, that coroutine terminates immediately, and so the client sees a single "Working" event.  So, we need to wait, more-or-less forever.  We can do that by [CODE] yield from [/CODE] ing an [CODE] asyncio.Future() [/CODE] . The second issue is that we'll somehow need to get a hold of all of the separate instances of a [CODE] SSEHandler() [/CODE] and use the [CODE] send() [/CODE] method on each of them, somehow.  Well, we can have each one self-register in their [CODE] handle_request() [/CODE] methods; by adding each one to a dict which maps the individual handler instances to the future they are waiting on. [CODE] class SSEHandler(sse.Handler):
    _instances = {}

    @asyncio.coroutine
    def handle_request(self):
        self.send('Working')
        my_future = asyncio.Future()
        SSEHandler._instances[self] = my_future
        yield from my_future
 [/CODE] Now, to send an event to every listening we just visit all of the SSEHandler instances registered in the dict we created and using [CODE] send() [/CODE] on each one. [CODE] class SSEHandler(sse.Handler):

    #...

    @classmethod
    def broadcast(cls, message):
        for instance, future in cls._instances.items():
            instance.send(message)

class Telnet(asyncio.Protocol):

    #...

    def data_received(self, data):
        #...
        SSEHandler.broadcast(data.decode('ascii'))
 [/CODE] lastly, your code exits when the telnet connection closes.  that's fine, but we should clean-up at that time, too.  Fortunately, that's just a matter of setting a result on all of the futures for all of the handlers [CODE] class SSEHandler(sse.Handler):

    #...

    @classmethod
    def abort(cls):
        for instance, future in cls._instances.items():
            future.set_result(None)
        cls._instances = {}

class Telnet(asyncio.Protocol):

    #...

    def connection_lost(self, esc):
        print("Connection lost!")
        SSEHandler.abort()
        telnet_server.close()
