Question: improved sprintf for PHP

improved sprintf for PHP Does anyone know a better implementation of sprintf in PHP ? I was looking for something like the string formatting we have in python: [CODE] print "Hello %(name)s. Your %(name)s has just been created!" % { 'name' : 'world' }
# prints::: Hello world. Your world has just been created!
 [/CODE] This is pretty handy to avoid repeating the same variables without need, such as: [CODE] sprintf("Hello %s. Your %s has just been created!", 'world', 'world');
# prints::: Hello world. Your world has just been created!
 [/CODE] I guess is fairly easy to build this on my own, but don't wanna reinvent the wheel, if you know what I mean... but I could not find (maybe wrong search keywords) any trace of this anywhere. If anyone can help, I appreciate. Cheers,

Answer: You can use positional (but not named) arguments to do this, for example [CODE] printf('Hello %1$s. Your %1$s has just been created!', 'world'); 
 [/CODE] A word of caution here: you must use single quotes, otherwise the dollar signs will cause PHP to try to substitute [CODE] $s [/CODE] with the value of this variable (which does not exist). If you want named arguments then you will have to do this with a regular expression; for example, see (php) Function that replaces texts with variables .

question:
How to except SyntaxError? I would like to except the error the following code produces, but I don't know how. [CODE] from datetime import datetime

try:
    date = datetime(2009, 12a, 31)
except:
    print "error"
 [/CODE] The code above is not printing [CODE] "error" [/CODE] . That's what I would like to be able to do. edit: The reason I would like to check for syntax errors, is because 12a is a command line parameter. Thanks.

answer:
command-line "parameters" are strings. if your code is: [CODE] datetime(2009, '12a', 31)
 [/CODE] it won't produce [CODE] SyntaxError [/CODE] . It raises [CODE] TypeError [/CODE] . All command-line parameters are needed to be cleaned up first, before use in your code. for example like this: [CODE] month = '12'
try:
    month = int(month)
except ValueError:
    print('bad argument for month')
    raise
else:
    if not 1<= month <= 12:
        raise ValueError('month should be between 1 to 12')
 [/CODE]

