question:
Standard Python wrapper to turn f(x) into f(*x)? I keep coming across use cases for the following wrapper: [CODE] def asterisk(fn):
   def retfn(x):
      return fn(*x)
   return retfn
 [/CODE] Is there something in the standard Python 2 library that already does this? I had a look in [CODE] functools [/CODE] , but couldn't find anything. For context, here is a recent use case: [CODE] print map(asterisk(operator.sub), [[-20, 20], [-20, 20], [32, 32]])
 [/CODE]

str.format() problem So I made this class that outputs '{0}' when x=0 or '{1}' for every other value of x. [CODE] class offset(str):  
    def __init__(self,x):  
        self.x=x  
    def__repr__(self):
        return repr(str({int(bool(self.x))}))
    def end(self,end_of_loop):
    #ignore this def it works fine
        if self.x==end_of_loop:
            return '{2}'
        else:
            return self
 [/CODE] I want to do this: [CODE] offset(1).format('first', 'next') [/CODE] but it will only return the number I give for x as a string.  What am I doing wrong?

answer:
Your subclass of [CODE] str [/CODE] does not override [CODE] format [/CODE] , so when you call [CODE] format [/CODE] on one of its instances it just uses the one inherited from [CODE] str [/CODE] which uses [CODE] self [/CODE] 's "intrinsic value as [CODE] str [/CODE] ", i.e., the string form of whatever you passed to [CODE] offset() [/CODE] . To change that intrinsic value you might override [CODE] __new__ [/CODE] , e.g.: [CODE] class offset(str):
    def __init__(self, x):
        self.x = x
    def __new__(cls, x):
        return str.__new__(cls, '{' + str(int(bool(x))) + '}')

for i in (0, 1):
  x = offset(i)
  print x
  print repr(x)
  print x.format('first', 'next')
 [/CODE] emits [CODE] {0}
'{0}'
first
{1}
'{1}'
next
 [/CODE] Note there's no need to also override [CODE] __repr__ [/CODE] if, by overriding [CODE] __new__ [/CODE] , you're already ensuring that the instance's intrinsic value as [CODE] str [/CODE] is the format you desire.

