question:
How is this called and how can be done ( `function_name.decorator` )? Really sorry for the extremely stupid title, but if I know what it is, I wouldn't write here (: [CODE] def some_decorator( func ):
    # ..

class A:
    @some_decorator
    def func():
        pass
    @func.some_decorator    # this one here - func.some_decorator ?
    def func():
        pass
 [/CODE] [CODE] some_decorator [/CODE] decorates [CODE] func [/CODE] - that's OK. But what is [CODE] func.some_decorator [/CODE] and how [CODE] some_decorator [/CODE] becomes a member ( or something else ? ) of [CODE] func [/CODE] ? P.S. I'm 90% sure, that there's such question here (as this seems something basic), but I don't know how to search it. If there's a exact duplicate, I'll delete this question. Note : It's not typo, nor accident, that both member functions are named [CODE] func [/CODE] . The decorator is for overloading: the question is related to Decorating method (class methods overloading)

answer:
Remember that the function definition with decorator is equivalent to this: [CODE] def func():
    pass
func = some_decorator(func)
 [/CODE] So in the following lines, [CODE] func [/CODE] doesn't refer to the function you defined but to what the decorator turned it into. Also note that decorators can return any object, not just functions. So [CODE] some_decorator [/CODE] returns an object with a method (it's unfortunate that the names [CODE] some_decorator [/CODE] and [CODE] func [/CODE] are reused in the example - it's confusing, but doesn't change anything about the concept) that is itself a decorator. As the expression after the [CODE] @ [/CODE] is evaluated first, you still have a reference to the first decorator's method after you defined another plain function [CODE] func [/CODE] . That decorator is applied to this new function. The full example is then equivalent to this: [CODE] class A:
    def func():
        pass
    func = some_decorator(func)

    _decorator = func.some_decorator
    def func():
        pass
    func = _decorator(func)
 [/CODE]

