I would simply add a method to each model [CODE] is_owned_by(user) [/CODE] , and it is upto the model to decide if it is owned by that user or not. In most case [CODE] is_owned_by [/CODE] can be a generic function in a base model class and you can tweak it in special cases. e.g. [CODE] class RentalPhoto(BaseModel):
    def is_owned_by(self, user):
        return self.rental.is_owned_by(user)
 [/CODE] This is generic enough and being explicit you will have full control how things behave. To add new permission you can add that to your models e.g. [CODE] class Rental(models.Model):
    # ...
    class Meta:
        permissions = (
            ("can_edit_any", "Can edit any rentals"),
        )
 [/CODE] I think instead of adding two permission for [CODE] any [/CODE] and [CODE] own [/CODE] , you should add only [CODE] own [/CODE] permission , so each object already has [CODE] can_edit [/CODE] which you can treat as user can edit only his object, and if user has permission can_edit_any than only he is allowed to edit all Using this we can extend auth by adding a custom backend e.g. [CODE] class PerObjectBackend(ModelBackend):

    def has_perm(self, user_obj, perm, obj=None):
        allowed = ModelBackend.has_perm(self, user_obj, perm)
        if perm.find('any') >=0 :
            return allowed

        if perm.find('edit') >=0 or perm.find('delete') >=0:
            if obj is None:
                raise Exception("Perm '%s' needs an object"%perm)
            if not obj.is_owned_by(user_obj):
                return False

        return allowed
 [/CODE] This is a very quick implemenation, in reality you can extend permission objects to check if it needs and object or not e.g. [CODE] permission.is_per_object [/CODE] instead of doing crude string search but that should also work if you have standard names

