There are two ways to do it. The direct approach would be to simply call (and print) the strftime() method in your template, for example [CODE] {{ car.date_of_manufacture.strftime('%Y-%m-%d') }}
 [/CODE] Another, sightly better approach would be to define your own filter, e.g.: [CODE] def format_datetime(value, format='medium'):
    if format == 'full':
        format="EEEE, d. MMMM y 'at' HH:mm"
    elif format == 'medium':
        format="EE dd.MM.y HH:mm"
    return babel.dates.format_datetime(value, format)

jinja_env.filters['datetime'] = format_datetime
 [/CODE] (This filter is based on babel for reasons regarding i18n, but you can use strftime too). The advantage of the filter is, that you can write [CODE] {{ car.date_of_manufacture|datetime }}
 [/CODE] which looks nicer and is more maintainable. Another common filter is also the "timedelta" filter, which evaluates to something like "written 8 minutes ago". You can use [CODE] babel.dates.format_timedelta [/CODE] for that, and register it as filter similar to the datetime example given here.

