Properties work using the descriptor protocol , which only works on attributes of a class object . The property object has to be stored in a class attribute. You can't "override" it on a per-instance basis. You can, of course, provide a property on the class that gets an instance attribute or falls back to some default: [CODE] class C(object):
    _default_x = 5
    _x = None
    @property
    def x(self):
        return self._x or self._default_x
    def alpha(self, beta):
        self._x = beta
 [/CODE]

