answer:
You don't actually need to get data from an [CODE] ImmutableMultiDict [/CODE] . There are a couple of errors in what you have that are preventing you from just pulling the response as json data. First off, you have to slightly tweak the parameters of your ajax call. You should add in the call type as a [CODE] POST [/CODE] . Furthermore, [CODE] datatype [/CODE] should be spelt as [CODE] dataType [/CODE] . Your new call should be: [CODE] var data = {"name":"John Doe","age":"21"};
$.ajax({
    type: 'POST',
    contentType: 'application/json',
    url: '/post/data',
    dataType : 'json',
    data : JSON.stringify(data),
    success : function(result) {
      jQuery("#clash").html(result); 
    },error : function(result){
       console.log(result);
    }
});
 [/CODE] The data is now actually being sent as a post request with the [CODE] json [/CODE] type. On the Flask server, we can now read the data as son information as follows: [CODE] @app.route('/post/data',methods=['GET','POST'])
def postdata():
    jsonData = request.get_json()
    print jsonData['name']
    print jsonData['age']
    return "hello world" #or whatever you want to return
 [/CODE] This will print [CODE] John Doe [/CODE] and [CODE] 21 [/CODE] successfully. Let me know if this works for you or if you have any additional questions! Edit: You can return success to the ajax call from flask as follows: [CODE] # include this import at the tomb
from flask import jsonify

@app.route('/post/data',methods=['GET','POST'])
    def postdata():
        ...
        return jsonify(success=True, data=jsonData)
 [/CODE]

