Python functions are 'executed' when the source code comes to them: [CODE] def foo():
    return bar

def bar():
    return 'spam'
 [/CODE] Here [CODE] foo [/CODE] depends on [CODE] bar [/CODE] , but it doesn't matter that the [CODE] bar [/CODE] function definition is not executed until after [CODE] foo [/CODE] has been declared. Only when you then call [CODE] foo() [/CODE] does the name [CODE] bar [/CODE] need to exist. In C however, you cannot do the above without an additional prototype declaration: [CODE] char * bar(void);

char * foo(void) {
    return bar();
}

char * bar(void) {
    return "spam";
}
 [/CODE] So yes, you can postpone the full declaration of [CODE] bar [/CODE] but the compiler demands that you still declare the function signature up front. So Python doesn't care whether or not [CODE] bar [/CODE] is defined until runtime, C requires functions to be declared at compile time.

