question:
Reversing default dict I have a defaultdict(list) where my keys are of type tuple and my values are a list of tuples.
I want to reverse this dictionary and I have already tried using the zip function and this doesn't work A sample of my dictionary structure is below [CODE] {(2, '-', 3): [('Canada', 'Trudeau'),('Obama', 'USA')]}
 [/CODE] Is there anyway to reverse this so I get the keys and values the other way round

answer:
Since you have a dict of lists, you need to make the lists an immutable type to use as a key in a Python dict. You can make the list values tuples: [CODE] >>> di={(2, '-', 3): [('Canada', 'Trudeau'),('Obama', 'USA')]}
>>> {tuple(v):k for k, v in di.items()}
{(('Canada', 'Trudeau'), ('Obama', 'USA')): (2, '-', 3)}
 [/CODE] Or turn them into strings: [CODE] >>> {repr(v):k for k, v in di.items()}
{"[('Canada', 'Trudeau'), ('Obama', 'USA')]": (2, '-', 3)}
 [/CODE] With either, you can turn the key back into a list if need be: [CODE] >>> from ast import literal_eval
>>> literal_eval("[('Canada', 'Trudeau'), ('Obama', 'USA')]")
[('Canada', 'Trudeau'), ('Obama', 'USA')]

>>> list((('Canada', 'Trudeau'), ('Obama', 'USA')))
[('Canada', 'Trudeau'), ('Obama', 'USA')]
 [/CODE] (BTW: Don't use [CODE] eval [/CODE] if code that might have any outside strings -- use ast.literal_eval in production code as in the example here.) Lastly, consider this dict: [CODE] di={'key 1':'val', 'key 2': 'val', 'key 3': 'val'}
 [/CODE] When you reverse the keys and values you will loose data as each key 'val' is added. You can use a default dict to solve duplicate values becoming keys: [CODE] dd=defaultdict(list)
for k, v in di.items():
    dd[v].append(k)

>>> dd
defaultdict(<type 'list'>, {'val': ['key 1', 'key 2', 'key 3']})
 [/CODE]

