This is one of the edge cases in Python: Everything in Python is an object, so since [CODE] object [/CODE] is the base type of everything, [CODE] type [/CODE] (being something in Python) is an instance of [CODE] object [/CODE] . Since [CODE] object [/CODE] is the base type of everything, [CODE] object [/CODE] is also a type , which makes [CODE] object [/CODE] an instance of [CODE] type [/CODE] . Note that this relationship is nothing you can replicate with your own things in Python. Itâs a single exception that is built into the language. On the implementation side, the two names are represented by [CODE] PyBaseObject_Type [/CODE] (for [CODE] object [/CODE] ) and [CODE] PyType_Type [/CODE] (for [CODE] type [/CODE] ). When you use [CODE] isinstance [/CODE] , the type checkâin the very last step, after everything else has failedâis done by [CODE] type_is_subtype_base_chain [/CODE] : [CODE] type_is_subtype_base_chain(PyTypeObject *a, PyTypeObject *b)
{
    do {
        if (a == b)
            return 1;
        a = a->tp_base;
    } while (a != NULL);

    return (b == &PyBaseObject_Type);
}
 [/CODE] This essentially keeps going up the type hierarchy of [CODE] a [/CODE] and checks the resulting type against [CODE] b [/CODE] . If it cannot find one, then the last resort is to check whether [CODE] b [/CODE] is actually [CODE] object [/CODE] in which case the function returns true: since everything is an object. So the âeverything is an instance of [CODE] object [/CODE] â part is actually hardcoded into the instance check. And as for why [CODE] object [/CODE] is a [CODE] type [/CODE] , this is actually even simpler because itâs simply defined that way in the declaration of [CODE] PyBaseObject_Type [/CODE] : [CODE] PyTypeObject PyBaseObject_Type = {
    PyVarObject_HEAD_INIT(&PyType_Type, 0)
    "object",                                   /* tp_name */
    sizeof(PyObject),                           /* tp_basicsize */
    â¦
 [/CODE] The [CODE] PyVarObject_HEAD_INIT [/CODE] essentially sets the core type information stuff, including the base type, which is [CODE] PyType_Type [/CODE] . There are actually two more consequences of this relationship: Since everything is an object, [CODE] object [/CODE] is also an instance of [CODE] object [/CODE] : [CODE] isinstance(object, object) [/CODE] Since [CODE] PyType_Type [/CODE] is also implemented with the same [CODE] PyVarObject_HEAD_INIT [/CODE] , [CODE] type [/CODE] is also a type: [CODE] isinstance(type, type) [/CODE] .

