How should I expose read-only fields from Python classes? I have many different small classes which have a few fields each, e.g. this: [CODE] class Article:
    def __init__(self, name, available):
        self.name = name
        self.available = available
 [/CODE] What's the easiest and/or most idiomatic way to make the [CODE] name [/CODE] field read only, so that [CODE] a = Article("Pineapple", True)
a.name = "Banana"  # <-- should not be possible
 [/CODE] is not possible anymore? Here's what I considered so far: Use a getter (ugh!). [CODE] class Article:
    def __init__(self, name, available):
        self._name = name
        self.available = available

    def name(self):
        return self._name
 [/CODE] Ugly, non-pythonic - and a lot of boilerplate code to write (especially if I have multiple fields to make read-only). However, it does the job and it's easy to see why that is. Use [CODE] __setattr__ [/CODE] : [CODE] class Article:
    def __init__(self, name, available):
        self.name = name
        self.available = available

    def __setattr__(self, name, value):
        if name == "name":
            raise Exception("%s property is read-only" % name)
        self.__dict__[name] = value
 [/CODE] Looks pretty on the caller side, seems to be the idiomatic way to do the job - but unfortunately I have many classes with only a few fields to make read only each. So I'd need to add a [CODE] __setattr__ [/CODE] implementation to all of them. Or use some sort of mixin maybe? In any case, I'd need to make up my mind how to behave in case a client attempts to assign a value to a read-only field. Yield some exception, I guess - but which? Use a utility function to define properties (and optionally getters) automatically. This is basically the same idea as (1) except that I don't write the getters explicitely but rather do something like [CODE] class Article:
    def __init__(self, name, available):
        # This function would somehow give a '_name' field to s

answer:
I would use [CODE] property [/CODE] as a decorator to manage your getter for [CODE] name [/CODE] (see the example for the class [CODE] Parrot [/CODE] in the documentation). Use, for example, something like: [CODE] class Article(object):
    def __init__(self, name, available):
        self._name = name
        self.available = available

    @property
    def name(self):
        return self._name
 [/CODE] If you do not define the setter for the [CODE] name [/CODE] property (using the decorator [CODE] x.setter [/CODE] around a function) this throws an [CODE] AttributeError [/CODE] when you try and reset [CODE] name [/CODE] . Note : You have to use Python's new-style classes (i.e. in Python 2.6 you have to inherit from [CODE] object [/CODE] ) for properties to work correctly. This is not the case according to @SvenMarnach.

