No, co-routines do not involve any kind of threads. Co-routines allow for cooperative multi-tasking in that each co-routine yields control voluntarily. Threads on the other hand switch between units at arbitrary points. Up to Python 3.4, it was possible to write co-routines using generators ; by using [CODE] yield [/CODE] or [CODE] yield from [/CODE] expressions in a function body you create a generator object instead, where code is only executed when you iterate over the generator. Together with additional event loop libraries (such as [CODE] asyncio [/CODE] ) you could write co-routines that would signal to an event loop that they were going to be busy (waiting for I/O perhaps) and that another co-routine could be run in the meantime: [CODE] import asyncio
import datetime

@asyncio.coroutine
def display_date(loop):
    end_time = loop.time() + 5.0
    while True:
        print(datetime.datetime.now())
        if (loop.time() + 1.0) >= end_time:
            break
        yield from asyncio.sleep(1)
 [/CODE] Every time the above code advances to the [CODE] yield from asyncio.sleep(1) [/CODE] line, the event loop is free to run a different co-routine, because this routine is not going to do anything for the next second anyway . Because generators can be used for all sorts of tasks, not just co-routines, and because writing a co-routine using generator syntax can be confusing to new-comers, the PEP introduces new syntax that makes it clearer that you are writing a co-routine. With the PEP implemented, the above sample could be written instead as: [CODE] async def display_date(loop):
