Question: Pickling inner classes

Pickling inner classes I have a python module called [CODE] model [/CODE] with basically the following content: [CODE] class Database:

    class Publiation(object):
        pass

    class Article(Publication):
        pass

    class Book(Publication):
        pass

class AnotherDatabase:

    class Seminar(object):
        pass

    ...
 [/CODE] I define the objects in the database as classes nested under a main class in order to organize them more distinctively. The objects are parsed from a large XML file, which takes time. I would like to pickle the imported objects to make them loadable in shorter time. I get the error: pickle.PicklingError: Can't pickle
  : it's
  not found as project.model.Article The class is now [CODE] project.model.Article [/CODE] , not [CODE] project.model.Database.Article [/CODE] as defined. Can I fix this error and keep the classes nested like above? Is it a bad idea to organize classes by nesting them?

Answer: When an inner class is created, there is no way for the interpreter to know which class it was defined inside of, this information is not recorded. This is why pickle does not know where to look for the class Article. Because of this there are numerous issues when using inner classes, not just when it comes to pickling. If there are classes at the module scope with the same name, it introduces a lot of ambiguity as there is no easy way to tell the two types apart (e.g. with repr or when debugging.) As a result it is generally best to avoid nested classes in Python unless you have a very good reason for doing so.

question:
How to try several methods with flat style? If I want to try many way to avoid some error, I may write: [CODE] try:

    try:
        trial_1()
    except some_error:
        try:
            trial_2()
        except some_error:
            try:
                trial_3()
            ...
    print "finally pass"

except some_error:
    print "still fail"
 [/CODE] But there are too many trials so too many nest, how to write it in a flat style?

answer:
If it's the same exception each time, you could do [CODE] for task in (trial_1, trial_2, trial_3, ...):
    try:
        task()
        break
    except some_error:
        continue
 [/CODE] If knowing whether it succeeded is important, the clearest way to add that is probably [CODE] successful = False
for task in (trial_1, trial_2, trial_3, ...):
    try:
        task()
        successful = True
        break
    except some_error:
        continue
if successful:
    ...
else:
    ...
 [/CODE]

