question:
double to PyFloat conversion is incorrect I'm learning SWIG, for using C in Python. I've written this function, but I can't understand, why the wrapped [CODE] myfunc [/CODE] returns wrong float/double values: mfuncs.c [CODE] #include <stdlib.h>

float myfunc(int n) {
    float result;
    result = 100 / n;
    return result;
}
 [/CODE] mfuncs.i [CODE] %module mfuncs
%typemap(out) double, float "$result = PyFloat_FromDouble($1);"

extern float myfunc(int n);
 [/CODE] Finally I get 1107558400.0 instead of 33.33333. >>> import mfuncs
>>> mfuncs.myfunc(3)
1107558400.0
>>> Where is the mistake?

answer:
The SWIG typemap is unneeded - it's provided by default, you only need to write typemaps for "esoteric" types and the default [CODE] double [/CODE] / [CODE] float [/CODE] ones  provided are fine here. The real problem here is that you're not compiling with warnings enabled or ignoring them! It's really worth getting in the habit of compiling with "-Wall -Wextra" or whatever your compiler requires to enable maximum warnings and heeding them. Your SWIG interface only tells SWIG about the function [CODE] myfunc [/CODE] but there's nothing in that interface to make the declaration available to the compiler you use to compile the generated myfuncs_wrap.c. This means that when you come to compile the shared library you're relying on an implicit declaration of [CODE] myfunc [/CODE] . GCC on my machine with -Wall reports this: test_wrap.c:3139:3: warning: implicit declaration of function 'myfunc' The implicit declaration assumes it returns [CODE] int [/CODE] . That's just the rule in C if there is no declaration, it's as though you wrote: [CODE] #include <stdlib.h>

int myfunc(int n);

int main() {
  printf("%d\n", myfunc(3));   
  return 0;
}
 [/CODE] which is clearly wrong (undefined behaviour to be exact) given the definition of [CODE] myfunc [/CODE] returns a [CODE] float [/CODE] . Your implementation (legally) chooses to do the simplest thing for this undefined behaviour, which is roughly a bit-wise cast from [CODE] int [/CODE] to [CODE] float [/CODE] . (It could equally well do anything , even something different on every run - that's the beauty of undefined behaviour). You can fix your SWIG interface by changing it to: [CODE] %module mfuncs
%{
extern float myfunc(int n);
%}

extern float myfunc(int n);
 [/CODE] this works because the code between [CODE] %{ [/CODE] and [CODE] %} [/CODE] is directly passed to the generated wrapper, which makes the compiler aware of the real declaration of [CODE] myfunc [/CODE] when building the wrapper. There's a nicer solution in my view though: provide the declaration only once, in a header file and then your interface file becomes: [CODE] %module mfuncs
%{
#include "myfunc.h"
%}

%include "myfunc.h"
 [/CODE] (and obviously [CODE] #include "myfunc.h" [/CODE] in myfunc.c). In that way you only write the declaration once and the compiler will warn/error if there's anything that's not quite expected rather than just take a (usually wrong) best guess.

