answer:
You are placing only the last replacement result in [CODE] newstr [/CODE] . All your previous [CODE] str.replace() [/CODE] results are discarded. For your input text [CODE] Hey look Words! [/CODE] , the last vowel encountered is [CODE] o [/CODE] so only [CODE] o [/CODE] is replaced. The [CODE] e [/CODE] replacement did take place and was stored in [CODE] newstr [/CODE] but that value was then discarded when you set [CODE] newstr [/CODE] to the result of the [CODE] o [/CODE] replacement. It thus depends on the input string what vowel exactly will remain replaced; for the sentence [CODE] 'The cat sat on the mat' [/CODE] it'll be [CODE] a [/CODE] as that is the last vowel you test and replace. Just loop directly over [CODE] vowels [/CODE] and replace each of those characters; it is save to call [CODE] str.replace() [/CODE] where the first argument is not present. Store the result back in [CODE] text [/CODE] so that any subsequent replacements stick: [CODE] def anti_vowel(text):
    vowels = "AaEeOoIiUu"
    for vowel in vowels:
        text = text.replace(vowel, "")
    return text
 [/CODE] Better still, use the [CODE] str.translate() [/CODE] method to replace all vowels in one go: [CODE] # Python 2 version
def anti_vowel(text):
    vowels = "AaEeOoIiUu"
    return text.translate(None, vowels)

# Python 3 version
def anti_vowel(text):
    vowels = str.maketrans(dict.fromkeys("AaEeOoIiUu"))
    return text.translate(vowels)
 [/CODE] [CODE] str.translate() [/CODE] makes all replacements at once; the method changed between Python 2 [CODE] str [/CODE] and Python 3 [CODE] str [/CODE] , but in both versions all the vowels are ignored as the new string is built, without any further loops.

