How to parse a tuple of "x=y" strings into a dictionary in python? I have a tuple of strings that I would like to convert into a dictionary by splitting each element using a separator. Is this possible with dictionary comprehension? [CODE] >>> x = ('var1=abc', 'var2=xyz', 'var3=10')
>>> y = {k: v for (k, v) in ???}  # this is where I need your help
>>> print y
{ "var1": "abc", "var2": "xyz", "var3": "10" }
 [/CODE] This is as much an learning exercise as anything - so please don't answer with an alternative way of doing it (I have plenty of those) - I want to know if it's possible with a dictionary comprehension. [UPDATE] There is a slight modification that I didn't write down first - I need to strip an extraneous linefeed character from the end of each value. [CODE] >>> x = ('var1=abc\n', 'var2=xyz\n', 'var3=10')
>>> d = {k: v.strip('\n') for (k, v) in [e.split('=') for e in x]}
>>> d
{'var1': 'abc', 'var3': '10', 'var2': 'xyz'}
 [/CODE] (Of course, now it's not in the same order - but I can live with that. Grrr.)

answer:
You'd have to split each string twice, but yes, it can be done: [CODE] {elem.split('=', 1)[0]: elem.split('=', 1)[1].strip() for elem in x} 
 [/CODE] Result: [CODE] >>> x = ('var1=abc\n', 'var2=xyz\n', 'var3=10')
>>> {elem.split('=', 1)[0]: elem.split('=', 1)[1].strip() for elem in x} 
{'var1': 'abc', 'var3': '10', 'var2': 'xyz'}
 [/CODE] To avoid splitting twice, use a generator expression and the [CODE] dict() [/CODE] constructor instead: [CODE] dict(elem.strip().split('=', 1) for elem in x)
 [/CODE] since it takes a sequence of two-value tuples.

