class Arr{
public:
    int n_rows;
    int n_cols;
    float* m_data;

    Arr(int r, int c, float v);
    virtual ~Arr();
    float get(int i, int j);
    void set(int i, int j, float v);

    long data_addr(){
        return (long)(m_data);
    }
};
 [/CODE] Constraints: I only care about classes that store their underlying data as contiguous flat arrays, The class will provide public access to the raw storage (probably through a function), I cannot add python specific code to the C++ header/source files (we do not want 
to have a Python dependency for the C++ code) thus any modifications on the C++
side have to be done via SWIG (e.g. [CODE] %extend [/CODE] ). My current approach is to put a [CODE] pythoncode [/CODE] block in my SWIG [CODE] .i [/CODE] file
that looks something like [CODE] %pythoncode{
def arraylike_getitem(self, arg1,arg2 ):
   # the actual implementation to handle slices
   # is pretty complicated but involves:
   # 1. constructing an uninitialized  numpy array for return value
   # 2. iterating over the indices indicated by the slices,
   # 3. calling self.getValue for each of the index pairs,
   # 4. returning the array

# add the function to the ArrayLike class
Arr.__getitem__=arraylike_getitem
%}
 [/CODE] where [CODE] ArrayLike [/CODE] is the C++ class that holds the numerical data (as a flat array),
and provides member functions to get/set individual values. The main drawback is step 1. above: I have to make a copy of any slice
that I take of my c-array class.  (The main advantage is that by returning a
numpy array object, I know that I can use it in any numpy operations that I want.) I can imagine two approaches for improving this: Adding (via SWIG [CODE] %extend [/CODE] ) additional functionality to the c class, and or

answer:
If [CODE] n_cols [/CODE] and [CODE] n_rows [/CODE] are (effectively) immutable, your best course of action is to simply create a real numpy array, giving it [CODE] m_data [/CODE] as storage and [CODE] (n_rows, n_cols) [/CODE] as shape. That way you will get all the numpy array facilities without any copying and without having to reimplement them in your own code (which would be a lot of quacking to imitate). [CODE] PyObject* array_like_to_numpy(ArrayLike& obj)
{
    npy_intp dims[] = { obj.n_rows, obj.n_cols };
    return PyArray_SimpleNewFromData(2, dims, NPY_FLOAT, obj.m_data);
}
 [/CODE] Of course, this won't work as written, since your [CODE] m_data [/CODE] member is protected. But it would be a good idea to either make it public or provide an accessor to retrieve it (or inherit from [CODE] ArrayLike [/CODE] and provide such functionality in your subclass).

