Answer: The other two answers allude to using metaclasses to accomplish what you want to do. To help learn about them, here's an example of applying one to the code in your question that makes it do what you want: [CODE] class _static_property(object):
    """ Descriptor class used for declaring computed properties that 
        don't require a class instance. """
    def __init__(self, getter, setter):
        self.getter = getter
        self.setter = setter

    def __get__(self, obj, objtype=None):
        print "In the getter function"
        return self.getter(obj)

    def __set__(self, obj, value):
        print "In setter function"
        self.setter(obj, value)

class _MyMetaClass(type):
    def getX(self):
        return self._x

    def setX(self, v):
        self._x = v

    X = _static_property(getX, setX)

class MyTest(object):
    __metaclass__ = _MyMetaClass
    _x = 42

print MyTest.__dict__
print MyTest.X
MyTest.X = 12
print MyTest.X
print MyTest.__dict__
 [/CODE] Classes are instances of their metaclass which is normally type [CODE] type [/CODE] . However you can use that as a base class and derive your own specialized meta-subclass -- that in this case is one that has class attributes which are data descriptors (aka properties). Note that the [CODE] self [/CODE] argument in a meta- or submetaclass method is a metaclass instance, which in code above is the class named [CODE] MyTest [/CODE] . Hope this helps.

