Short Answer Unless you have a good reason you should use [CODE] InputRequired [/CODE] Why? Lets look at some notes from the docs/code: Note there is a distinction between this and DataRequired in that InputRequired looks that form-input data was provided, and DataRequired looks at the post-coercion data. and NOTE this validator used to be called [CODE] Required [/CODE] but the way it behaved (requiring coerced data, not input data) meant it functioned in a way which was not symmetric to the [CODE] Optional [/CODE] validator and furthermore caused confusion with certain fields which coerced data to 'falsey' values like [CODE] 0 [/CODE] , [CODE] Decimal(0) [/CODE] , [CODE] time(0) [/CODE] etc. Unless a very specific reason exists, we recommend using the :class: [CODE] InputRequired [/CODE] instead. what does this mean? In the [CODE] Form [/CODE] class you will notice two keyword arguments [CODE] formdata [/CODE] and [CODE] data [/CODE] . These generally correspond with two methods [CODE] process [/CODE] and [CODE] process_formdata [/CODE] . When form data comes in off the wire its not always in the format corresponding to the [CODE] Field [/CODE] type. A good example of this is the value [CODE] u'1' [/CODE] being supplied to an [CODE] IntegerField [/CODE] .  This would be bad news if you had a [CODE] NumberRange [/CODE] validator because [CODE] u'1' [/CODE] isn't a number. The primary purpose of the [CODE] process_formdata [/CODE] method is to prevent this situation by coercing the value into its correct type prior to running validation rules. That is what they are referring to when they say "looks at the post-coercion data" the problem! Both [CODE] InputRequired [/CODE] and [CODE] DataRequired [/CODE] work the same way specifically the [CODE] __call__ [/CODE] implementations: [CODE] def __call__(self, form, field):
    if not field.data or isinstance(field.data, string_types) and not field.data.strip():
        if self.message is None:
            message = field.gettext('This field is required.')
        else:
            message = self.message
 [/CODE] Certain field types coerce data into Falsey values(0, Decimal(0), etc.). The problem occurs when you have an [CODE] IntegerField [/CODE] and the form submits a value like [CODE] '0' [/CODE] . If you apply [CODE] DataRequired [/CODE] to this it will fail validation. This is because [CODE] DataRequired [/CODE] will evaluate [CODE] if not field.data... [/CODE] after coercion where [CODE] field.data [/CODE] is the Falsey numeric value [CODE] 0 [/CODE] .

