Answer: Unfortunately, at the moment, it is not possible to expose a [CODE] std::unique_ptr [/CODE] using boost::python, as it requires move semantics, and these are not supported by boost::python yet (more details here ). Moreover, you should not derive from smart pointers, because it is not a good practice and they do not have virtual destructors (apart from many other reasons) Saying that, you have quite a few other options, some of which are: use [CODE] std::auto_ptr [/CODE] as a data member, which is well supported by [CODE] boost::python [/CODE] (assuming your class is made non-copyable). However, an [CODE] auto_ptr [/CODE] cannot hold an array (it would not call the correct variant of [CODE] delete() [/CODE] ). use [CODE] boost::shared_ptr [/CODE] as a data member, and hide its use in the API. However, please note that [CODE] boost::shared_ptr [/CODE] cannot not hold array data either, for the same reason as above. wrap an [CODE] std::vector<boost::shared_ptr<>> [/CODE] , which will work with [CODE] boost::shared_ptr [/CODE] out of the box using vector_indexing_suite . ... and many others. However, there are many other ways, and I would personally recommend to redesign your code to use some of the more generally accepted coding and python-wrapping practices from boost::python examples.

