How do I send data from JS to Python with Flask? I'm making a website with Flask and I'd like to be able to execute python code using data from the page. I know that I can simply use forms but it's a single page that is continually updated as it receives user input and it'd be a massive pain in the ass to have it reload the page every time something happens. I know I can do [CODE] {{ function() }} [/CODE] inside the javascript but how do I do [CODE] {{ function(args) }} [/CODE] inside the javascript using js variables? So far the only thing I can think of is to update an external database like MongoDB with the js then use Python to read from that, but this process will slow down the website quite a lot. The jQuery needs to get a list of dictionary objects from the Python function which can then be used in the html. So I need to be able to do something like: JS: [CODE] var dictlist = { getDictList(args) };
dictlist.each(function() {
    $("<.Class>").text($(this)['Value']).appendTo("#element");
});
 [/CODE] Python: [CODE] def getDictList(args):
    return dictlistMadeFromArgs
 [/CODE]

answer:
To get data from Javascript to Python with Flask, you either make an AJAX POST request or AJAX GET request with your data. Flask has six HTTP methods available , of which we only need the GET and POST. Both will take [CODE] jsdata [/CODE] as a parameter, but get it in different ways. That's how two completely different languages in two different environments like Python and Javascript exchange data. First, instantiate a GET route in Flask: [CODE] @app.route('/getmethod/<jsdata>')
def get_javascript_data(jsdata):
    return jsdata
 [/CODE] or a POST one: [CODE] @app.route('/postmethod', methods = ['POST'])
def get_post_javascript_data():
    jsdata = request.form['javascript_data']
    return jsdata
 [/CODE] The first one is accessed by [CODE] /getmethod/<javascript_data> [/CODE] with an AJAX GET as follows: [CODE] $.get( "/getmethod/<javascript_data>" );
 [/CODE] The second one by using an AJAX POST request: [CODE] $.post( "/postmethod", {
    javascript_data: data 
});
 [/CODE] Where [CODE] javascript_data [/CODE] is either a JSON dict or a simple value. In case you choose JSON, make sure you convert it to a dict in Python: [CODE] json.loads(jsdata)[0]
 [/CODE] Eg. GET: [CODE] @app.route('/getmethod/<jsdata>')
def get_javascript_data(jsdata):
    return json.loads(jsdata)[0]
 [/CODE] POST: [CODE] @app.route('/postmethod', methods = ['POST'])
def get_post_javascript_data():
    jsdata = request.form['javascript_data']
    return json.loads(jsdata)[0]
 [/CODE] If you need to do it the other way around, pushing Python data down to Javascript, create a simple GET route without parameters that returns a JSON encoded dict: [CODE] @app.route('/getpythondata')
def get_python_data():
    return json.dumps(pythondata)
 [/CODE] Retrieve it from JQuery and decode it: [CODE] $.get("/getpythondata", function(data) {
    console.log($.parseJSON(data))
})
 [/CODE] The [CODE] [0] [/CODE] in [CODE] json.loads(jsdata)[0] [/CODE] is there because when you decode a JSON encoded dict in Python, you get a list with the single dict inside, stored at index 0, so your JSON decoded data looks like this: [CODE] [{'foo':'bar','baz':'jazz'}] #[0: {'foo':'bar','baz':'jazz'}]
 [/CODE] Since what we need is the just the dict inside and not the list, we get the item stored at index 0 which is the dict. Also, [CODE] import json [/CODE] .

