Python binding for C++ operator overloading I have a class similar to the following: [CODE] class A {
    vector<double> v;
    double& x(int i) { return v[2*i]; }
    double& y(int i) { return v[2*i+1]; }
    double x(int i) const { return v[2*i]; }
    double y(int i) const { return v[2*i+1]; }
}
 [/CODE] I want to have the following Python code work: [CODE] a = A()
a.x[0] = 4
print a.x[0]
 [/CODE] I was thinking of [CODE] __setattr__ [/CODE] and [CODE] __getattr__ [/CODE] , but not sure if it works. An alternative is to implement the following Python: [CODE] a = A()
a['x', 0] = 4
