Inheriting from decorated classes I'm trying to decorate a class with another class. I also want to inherit from the decorated class, but I get some errors. Here's my code: [CODE] class Decorator:
    def __init__(self, decorated):
        pass

@Decorator
class Foo:
    pass

class Goo(Foo):
    pass
 [/CODE] The error I get when I try to subclass from [CODE] Foo [/CODE] is this: Traceback (most recent call last): File "test.py", line 9, in class Goo(Foo): TypeError: __init__() takes exactly 2 positional arguments (4 given) By adding another init function to [CODE] Decorator [/CODE] ... [CODE] def __init__(self, *args):
    for arg in args:
        print(arg)
 [/CODE] ... I get the following output: <class '__main__.Foo'> Goo (<__main__.Decorator object at 0x010073B0>,) {'__module__': '__main__'} What are those parameters and how should I be using them inside [CODE] Decorator [/CODE] ?

Answer: I'll try to answer the "what are those parameters" question. This code: [CODE] @Decorator
class Foo:
    pass
 [/CODE] is equivalent to: [CODE] class Foo:
    pass
Foo = Decorator(Foo)
 [/CODE] This means that [CODE] Foo [/CODE] ends up being an instance of the [CODE] Decorator [/CODE] class instead of being a class. When you try to use this instance as a base of a class ( [CODE] Goo [/CODE] ), Python will have to determine a metaclass that will be used to create the new class. In this case it will use [CODE] Foo.__class__ [/CODE] which equals to [CODE] Decorator [/CODE] . Then it will call the metaclass with [CODE] (name, bases, dict) [/CODE] arguments and expect it to return a new class. This is how you end up with these arguments in [CODE] Decorator.__init__ [/CODE] . More about this can be found here: http://www.python.org/download/releases/2.2.3/descrintro/#metaclasses (particularly the "When a class statement is executed..." part)

