>>> y = {'b':10, 'c': 11}
>>> z = x.update(y)
>>> print z
None
>>> x
{'a': 1, 'b': 10, 'c': 11}
 [/CODE] How can I get that final merged dict in z, not x? (To be extra-clear, the last-one-wins conflict-handling of [CODE] dict.update() [/CODE] is what I'm looking for as well.)

answer:
In your case, what you can do is: [CODE] z = dict(x.items() + y.items())
 [/CODE] This will, as you want it, put the final dict in [CODE] z [/CODE] , and make the value for key [CODE] b [/CODE] be properly overridden by the second ( [CODE] y [/CODE] ) dict's value: [CODE] >>> x = {'a':1, 'b': 2}
