Read the documentation everything is explained well, the cleanest way to do this when you will submit your data using Ajax, you have to add a custom header X-CSRFToken, the token can be parsed from the cookies, should be enabled by the way. here is a link to the documentation The documentation is always the best place to find your questions, you just have to read mate, but anyway, here is an example: [CODE] // Using the jquery cookie plugin, you can get the csrf token like this 
var csrftoken = $.cookie('csrftoken');
 [/CODE] then in your javascript/Anuglar code to post or make ajax request, you ahve to add the [CODE] X-CSRFToken [/CODE] header before making the POST/PUT request: [CODE] // This HTTP mehtod dont need procetion since you will be reading and not pushing data
//   into the server
function csrfSafeMethod(method) {
    // these HTTP methods do not require CSRF protection
    return (/^(GET|HEAD|OPTIONS|TRACE)$/.test(method));
}

// This is for setting up the upcoming ajax request, here you add the header with the
//   csrftoken you got it from the cookies
$.ajaxSetup({
    beforeSend: function(xhr, settings) {
        if (!csrfSafeMethod(settings.type) && !this.crossDomain) {
            xhr.setRequestHeader("X-CSRFToken", csrftoken);
        }
    }
});
 [/CODE] NB: This is using jQuery, you can still do the same thing using Angular. After this you can make your Ajax call and Django will happily accept your request, again RTD please, everything is well explained, you have all of this code examples included.

