The question user2357112 linked, explains this in some way: Why can't I handle a KeyboardInterrupt in python? . The keyboard interrupt is raised asynchronously, so it does not immediately terminate the application. Instead, the Ctrl+C is handled in some kind of event loop that takes a while to get there. This unfortunately means that you cannot reliably catch the [CODE] KeyboardInterrupt [/CODE] in this case. But we can do some things to get there. As I explained yesterday, the exception that stops the [CODE] raw_input [/CODE] call is not the [CODE] KeyboardInterrupt [/CODE] but an [CODE] EOFError [/CODE] . You can easily verify this by changing your [CODE] bing [/CODE] function like this: [CODE] def bing():
    try:
        raw_input()
    except Exception as e:
        print(type(e))
