Cannot catch KeyboardInterrupt in command prompt twice? Today, I had to check how my script runs on the Windows command prompt [1] , when I noticed something weird. I was working on something similar to this, but this is enough to demonstrate the problem. Here's the code. [CODE] def bing():
    try:
        raw_input()
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        print 'This is what actually happened here!'

try:                     # pardon me for those weird strings
    bing()               # as it's consistent with everything in the chat room (see below)
    print 'Yoo hoo...'
except KeyboardInterrupt:
    print 'Nothing happens here too!'
 [/CODE] Here's the situation. When the script runs, it waits for the input and the user is supposed to press Ctrl + C to raise a [CODE] KeyboardInterrupt [/CODE] which would (should) be caught by the [CODE] except [/CODE] block within [CODE] bing() [/CODE] . So, this should be the actual output. And, this is what happens when I run it in my Ubuntu termi

Answer: The question user2357112 linked, explains this in some way: Why can't I handle a KeyboardInterrupt in python? . The keyboard interrupt is raised asynchronously, so it does not immediately terminate the application. Instead, the Ctrl+C is handled in some kind of event loop that takes a while to get there. This unfortunately means that you cannot reliably catch the [CODE] KeyboardInterrupt [/CODE] in this case. But we can do some things to get there. As I explained yesterday, the exception that stops the [CODE] raw_input [/CODE] call is not the [CODE] KeyboardInterrupt [/CODE] but an [CODE] EOFError [/CODE] . You can easily verify this by changing your [CODE] bing [/CODE] function like this: [CODE] def bing():
    try:
        raw_input()
    except Exception as e:
        print(type(e))
 [/CODE] You will see that the exception type thatâs printed is [CODE] EOFError [/CODE] and not [CODE] KeyboardInterrupt [/CODE] . You will also see that the [CODE] print [/CODE] did not even go through completely: There is no new line. Thatâs apparently because the output got interrupted by the interrupt which arrived just after the print statement wrote the exception type to stdout. You can see this also when you add a bit more stuff to the print: [CODE] def bing():
    try:
        raw_input()
    except EOFError as e:
        print 'Exception raised:', 'EOF Error'
 [/CODE] Note that Iâm using two separate arguments here for the print statement. When we execute this, we can see the âException raisedâ text, but the âEOF Errorâ wonât appear. Instead, the [CODE] except [/CODE] from the outer call will trigger and the keyboard interrupt is caught. Things get a bit more out of control in Python 3 though. Take this code: [CODE] def bing():
    try:
        input()
    except Exception as e:
        print('Exception raised:', type(e))

try:
    bing()
    print('After bing')
except KeyboardInterrupt:
    print('Final KeyboardInterrupt')
 [/CODE] This is pretty much exactly

