A little late but, I've been using tornado-redis . It works with tornado's ioloop and the [CODE] tornado.gen [/CODE] module Install tornadoredis It can be installed from pip [CODE] pip install tornadoredis
 [/CODE] or with setuptools [CODE] easy_install tornadoredis
 [/CODE] but you really shouldn't do that. You could also clone the repository and extract it. Then run [CODE] python setup.py build
python setup.py install
 [/CODE] Connect to redis The following code goes in your main.py or equivalent [CODE] redis_conn = tornadoredis.Client('hostname', 'port')
redis_conn.connect()
 [/CODE] redis.connect is called only once. It is a blocking call, so it should be called before starting the main ioloop. The same connection object is shared between all the handlers. You could add it to your application settings like [CODE] settings = {
    redis = redis_conn
}
app = tornado.web.Application([('/.*', Handler),],
                              **settings)
 [/CODE] Use tornadoredis The connection can be used in handlers at [CODE] self.settings['redis'] [/CODE] or it can be added as a property of the BaseHandler and subclass that class for other request handlers. [CODE] class BaseHandler(tornado.web.RequestHandler):

    @property
    def redis():
        return self.settings['redis']
 [/CODE] To communicate with redis, the [CODE] tornado.web.asynchronous [/CODE] and the [CODE] tornado.gen.engine [/CODE] decorators are used [CODE] class SomeHandler(BaseHandler):

    @tornado.web.asynchronous
    @tornado.gen.engine
    def get(self):
        foo = yield gen.Task(self.redis.get, 'foo')
        self.render('sometemplate.html', {'foo': foo}
 [/CODE] Extra information More examples and other features like connection pooling and pipelines can be found at the github repo.

