How is Python blocking signals while os.system("sleep...")? When I run this Python script with [CODE] os.system [/CODE] on Ubuntu 12.04: [CODE] import os, signal
signal.signal(signal.SIGABRT, lambda *args: os.write(2, 'HANDLER\n'))
print 'status=%r' % os.system('sleep 5')
 [/CODE] , and then I send SIGABRT to the script process many times within 5 seconds, I get the following output: [CODE] status=0
HANDLER
 [/CODE] This indicates that the signal delivery was blocked until [CODE] sleep 5 [/CODE] exited, and then only a single signal was delivered. However, with [CODE] subprocess.call [/CODE] : [CODE] import os, signal, subprocess
signal.signal(signal.SIGABRT, lambda *args: os.write(2, 'HANDLER\n'))
print 'cstatus=%r' % subprocess.call('sleep 5', shell=True)
 [/CODE] , all individual signals are delivered early: [CODE] HANDLER
HANDLER
HANDLER
cstatus=0
 [/CODE] To distinguish the magic in glibc from the magic in Python, I rewrote the Python script in C, so [CODE] os.system [/CODE] became system(3) : [CODE] #include <errno.h>
#include <signal.h>
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <unistd.h>
static void handler(int signum) { (void)signum; write(2, "HANDLER\n", 8); }
int main(int argc, char **argv) {
  int got;
  struct sigaction sa;
  (void)argc; (void)argv;
  memset(&sa, 0, sizeof sa);
  sa.sa_handler = handler;
  if (0 != sigaction(SIGABRT, &sa, NULL)) return 3;
  got = system("sleep 5");
  return !printf("system=0x%x\n", got);
}
 [/CODE] Signals got delivered early: [CODE] HANDLER
HANDLER
HANDLER
system=0x0
 [/CODE] So I inferred that the magic is in Python 2.7, not in eglibc. But where is the magic? Based on the strace output and looking at the [CODE] posix_system [/CODE] function in [CODE] Modules/posixmodule.c [/CODE] , I couldn't figure out how Python blocks the signal until [CODE] os.system [/CODE] returns. Relevant code from [CODE] Modules/posixmodule.c [/CODE] : [CODE] static PyObject *posix_system(PyObject *self, PyObject *ar

answer:
Maybe the magic is in PY_BEGIN_ALLOW_THREADS? The magic is mostly in [CODE] system [/CODE] itself. [CODE] system [/CODE] cannot return EINTR, so the libc implementation goes to pains to resume its [CODE] wait [/CODE] 'ing on the child process.  That means in your use of [CODE] os.system [/CODE] , control never returns to python until the underlying [CODE] system [/CODE] completes, and thus the python signal handling mechanics aren't invoked timely. [CODE] subprocess.call [/CODE] , however, is essentially doing this: [CODE] # Compare subprocess.py:Popen/_eintr_retry_call(os.waitpid, self.pid, 0)
while True:
  try:
    return os.waitpid(the_child_pid, 0)
  except OSError, e:
    if e.errno == errno.EINTR:  # signal.signal() handler already invoked
      continue
    raise
 [/CODE] Here control does return to python when the underlying [CODE] wait [/CODE] is interrupted.  The OSError/EINTR prompts python to see if any signals were tripped and, if so, to invoke the user-supplied code block associated with that signal.  (And that's how the interpreter adapts the system's signal semantics:  set a flag, and check it between "atomic" python operations, invoking the user's code if appropriate.)

